[VitePress](https://vitepress.dev) is a static site generator built on Vite and Vue. You write pages in Markdown, and its default theme adds a home page layout, navigation, a sidebar, and dark mode, which makes it a common choice for project documentation.

# How does the integration work?

[Appwrite Sites](/docs/products/sites) includes VitePress in its template library as **Vitepress**. When you create a site from the template, Appwrite builds the default VitePress starter from the [appwrite/templates-for-sites](https://github.com/appwrite/templates-for-sites/tree/main/vite/vitepress) repository and serves it as a static site on an Appwrite domain. If you connect a repository, every page you push to the production branch is built and published.

Appwrite applies these build settings to the template:

| Setting | Value |
| --- | --- |
| Framework | Vite |
| Install command | `npm i vitepress && npm install` |
| Build command | `npm run docs:build` |
| Output directory | `./.vitepress/dist` |
| Fallback file | `404.html` |
| Rendering | Static |

{% info title="Self-hosted Appwrite" %}
The template builds on the `node-22` runtime, which `_APP_SITES_RUNTIMES` enables by default. To create a repository from the template, configure [version control](/docs/advanced/self-hosting/configuration/version-control) for your instance first. **Connect later** deploys the template straight from its public GitHub source.
{% /info %}

# How to implement

To deploy VitePress with Appwrite Sites, there are several steps you must complete:

## Step 1: Create a site from the template

For this step, you must [create an account on Appwrite Cloud](/sign-up) or [self-host Appwrite](/docs/advanced/self-hosting) if you haven't already. In your Appwrite project, go to **Sites** and click **Create site**. Search the templates for **Vitepress** and select it.

Enter a **Site name** and a **Site ID**, then choose how to manage the code:

- **Connect your repository** creates a new repository from the template with your Git provider, such as GitHub, or connects one you already have, and redeploys on every push to the production branch. Choose this to write your own docs.
- **Connect later** deploys the template as it is. You can connect a repository afterwards from the site's **Settings** > **Git repository**.

Pick a subdomain under **Domain** and click **Deploy**. You can follow the build logs in the Console, and the site goes live on that domain when the build succeeds.

## Step 2: Write your docs

Clone the repository and edit the starter:

- `.vitepress/config.mts`: the site `title` and `description`, and the `themeConfig` for the top navigation (`nav`), the `sidebar`, and `socialLinks`.
- `index.md`: the home page. Its frontmatter uses the `home` layout with a `hero` and a list of `features`.
- `markdown-examples.md` and `api-examples.md`: sample pages that show VitePress Markdown extensions and runtime APIs. Replace them with your own pages and update the sidebar to match.

Run `npm run docs:dev` to preview locally, then commit and push to the production branch. Appwrite builds the change and activates the new deployment.

## Step 3: Add a custom domain

Open your site's **Domains** tab to serve your docs from your own domain, such as a `docs` subdomain. Follow [Add a custom domain](/docs/products/sites/domains#add-a-custom-domain) for the DNS records to create.

